<?php
/** Extra in-depth sections for the first 18 journal articles (slug => [[heading, body], ...]). Markup as in seed_blog_lib.php. */
function jm_blog_ext_1(): array {
    return [
    'jawai-leopard-safari-complete-guide' => [
      ['How to read the hills like a tracker', 'Leopards in Jawai rarely sit in the open for long. They choose rocks that give them warmth in the morning, shade by midday and a clear view of movement below. Look for dark shapes under overhangs, for a line of spots along a ridge, and for the way crows, langurs and peafowl look in one direction. A tracker will often stop the jeep and simply wait, because a leopard that has been seen once usually shows itself again within minutes. || Binoculars make a huge difference. Scan slowly from the base of a boulder upward, then across the skyline, rather than sweeping fast. Keep your eyes soft so that movement catches your attention.'],
      ['What a good sighting actually looks like', 'A “sighting” is not always a close portrait. Many are a leopard resting 100 metres away, a cub moving between rocks, or a cat crossing a track at dusk. Treat each as a gift. If you see an animal at distance, switch off the engine, stay seated and let it settle. The longer you wait quietly, the more natural its behaviour becomes. || Sightings that feel special often include behaviour: stretching on a rock, scent-marking, a mother calling to cubs or a slow stalk. These are not things you can schedule, which is why several drives beat one long one.'],
      ['Mistakes first-time visitors make', '- Booking only one drive and expecting a guaranteed sighting. || - Wearing bright clothes or strong perfume. || - Talking constantly, which hides the tracker’s alarm-call cues. || - Rushing to a new spot every few minutes instead of waiting. || - Packing too little for cold mornings. || Plan three or four drives, keep a relaxed mindset and read [what to pack](blog/what-to-pack-for-jawai-safari) before you arrive.']],

    'best-time-to-visit-jawai' => [
      ['Matching the season to your reason for coming', 'If you are travelling mainly for comfort, choose November to February. If you want long, open views of leopards, choose March to June, when dry grass thins the cover. If you love green scenery, quiet roads and moody photography, choose July to September. Birdwatchers should aim for December to February when winter visitors fill the dam. Each purpose has a season, so decide your priority first and then pick dates. || Compare the detailed guides to [Jawai in winter](blog/jawai-in-winter-december-january-guide), [summer](blog/jawai-in-summer-april-june-leopard-sightings) and the [monsoon](blog/jawai-in-monsoon-july-september).'],
      ['Festivals and crowds', 'Winter weekends, Diwali, Christmas and New Year are the busiest dates, with full hotels and limited safari slots. If you can travel on weekdays or in the shoulder weeks of October and March, you get most of the benefit with calmer roads and easier booking. Book early for peak weeks. See [long weekends and holidays](blog/jawai-long-weekends-and-holidays-book-early-guide).'],
      ['Weather details worth knowing', '- Winter dawns are cold in an open jeep, often in the single digits to low teens Celsius, while afternoons are warm. || - Summer afternoons can be extremely hot, so drives are early and late. || - Monsoon rains can occasionally limit tracks, so keep spare time in your plan. || - Fog is possible on winter mornings and usually clears quickly. || Whatever the month, carry layers, a hat and water.']],

    'how-to-reach-jawai-from-udaipur-jodhpur-jaipur' => [
      ['Choosing between Udaipur and Jodhpur as your gateway', 'Udaipur airport is about 129 km away and the drive to Bera is around two hours and 15 minutes. Jodhpur airport is about 173 km and nearer three hours. If you are flying in from Delhi or Mumbai, check both for the best schedule and fare. Udaipur is usually easier for a quick trip. Jodhpur suits those combining the Blue City and Mehrangarh Fort. Read our guides to [Jodhpur to Jawai](blog/jodhpur-to-jawai-road-trip-route-stops-timing) and [Udaipur in one day](blog/udaipur-sightseeing-after-jawai-safari).'],
      ['Doing it by train', 'The nearest stations are around Sumerpur, roughly 18 km from Bera, and Falna, a larger junction. Check the timetable and which trains stop at each station on IRCTC, and arrange a taxi for the last stretch. Arriving at night is better avoided; pick trains that reach by morning or afternoon. See [reaching Jawai by train and road](blog/reach-jawai-by-train-and-road-from-ahmedabad-mumbai-delhi).'],
      ['Driving yourself or hiring a driver', 'Highways are good, but rural stretches have cattle, tractors and few lights. A driver who knows the route is comfortable, especially for the last hour. If you drive yourself, leave early, fill fuel before the last stretch, keep some cash for tolls and avoid driving after dark. Share your arrival time with the hotel so someone can meet you.']],

    'where-to-stay-in-jawai-bera-hotels-camps' => [
      ['How to judge a stay near the hills', 'Location matters, but so do small things: how long to the first safari point, whether the staff can arrange drives for you, what the rooms are like in winter and summer, and whether meals suit your diet. Ask for current photos, check recent guest reviews and confirm what is included. A clean room, hot water, a reliable team and honest advice matter more than fancy extras in a village location.'],
      ['Camps, homestays and hotels compared', '- Tented camps: atmospheric and close to nature, but they can be cold in winter and hot in summer, and facilities vary. || - Homestays: personal and local, with a simpler standard. || - Hotels: more predictable comfort, hot water, air-conditioning and a restaurant, often easier for families and older guests. || Pick based on your comfort needs, not only photographs. See our [rooms](rooms) to compare.'],
      ['Questions to ask before you book', '- Is the safari included or arranged separately, and how is it priced? || - Is food vegetarian only, or is there choice? || - What is the cancellation policy? || - Is there Wi-Fi and a power backup? || - How far is the room from the village centre and the hills? || A good host answers plainly and does not promise sightings. Read [booking tips](blog/jawai-trip-booking-tips-changes-cancellations).']],

    'jawai-dam-jawai-bandh-history-birds-crocodiles' => [
      ['How the dam shapes the landscape', 'The reservoir turned a dry, rocky region into a place where water, farmland and wildlife meet. Villages draw on the water for farming, birds gather in huge numbers in winter and animals come to drink, including leopards from the nearby hills. The water edge changes with the season: wide and glassy after the monsoon, shrinking into mudflats and sandbars in summer, which is when many waders and crocodiles are easiest to see.'],
      ['The best ways to visit', 'A morning visit gives soft light and the most bird activity, while an evening visit gives sunset reflections. Go with your driver or guide rather than alone, and stay well back from the edge. Bring binoculars, a hat and water. Read about [sunrise and sunset spots](blog/best-sunrise-sunset-spots-around-jawai) and [marsh crocodiles at Jawai Dam](blog/marsh-crocodiles-and-wetland-at-jawai-dam).'],
      ['Planning it into a Jawai trip', 'Combine a dam visit with a leopard drive on the same morning, since the hills and water are close. Winter is best for migratory birds, summer for crocodiles on sandbars and the monsoon for a full, green reservoir. A good plan is a dawn safari followed by a slow stop at the water.']],

    'rabari-community-jawai-culture-and-leopards' => [
      ['A way of life built around movement', 'The Rabari are traditionally pastoral people who raise sheep, goats and camels and move with the seasons in search of grazing. Their knowledge of the land, water and animal behaviour is deep, passed down through families. Many now farm or work in other jobs as well, but herding remains a strong part of identity and culture.'],
      ['Why Rabari tolerance matters', 'Leopards sometimes take livestock, which is a real cost to herding families. Yet many Rabari have long shared these hills with leopards, treating them with a mix of caution and respect. This tolerance is a major reason Jawai’s leopards are as visible and secure as they are. Visitors can help sustain it by choosing responsible operators and spending money locally. Read [human–leopard coexistence](blog/human-leopard-coexistence-in-jawai-explained).'],
      ['Meeting people with respect', '- Ask before taking photographs, especially of women. || - Do not offer money for pictures; buy chai or a small item instead. || - Dress modestly and keep your voice low. || - Do not enter homes or pens without an invitation. || - Learn a few words, such as “ram ram” or “namaste”. || See [Rajasthani turbans and Rabari dress](blog/rajasthani-turban-safa-and-rabari-dress-explained).']],

    'jawai-3-day-itinerary-safari-kumbhalgarh-ranakpur' => [
      ['Why this order works', 'Starting with leopard drives gives you the best chance of success before you spend a day on the road. A rest day in the middle helps if the first sighting is quiet, and the heritage day trip comes last when you are ready for something different. Keep Day 3 flexible, because tiredness, weather or a sudden sighting may change your plan.'],
      ['Adjusting the plan to your pace', '- Slow pace: do one drive a day and add a village walk. || - Photography focus: replace the day trip with two more drives. || - Heritage lovers: add Mount Abu on a fourth day. || - Families: keep the day trip to Ranakpur only. || See [places near Jawai Bandh](blog/places-to-visit-near-jawai-bandh-distance-by-distance).'],
      ['Practical timing', 'Drives start around first light and about 4:00 PM. Ranakpur’s visitor hours for non-Jains are generally in the afternoon, so check before you go. Leave Kumbhalgarh by mid-afternoon to reach Bera before dark. Carry water, snacks and some cash for tolls and tickets. Read our [Ranakpur guide](blog/ranakpur-jain-temples-visit-guide-from-jawai).']],

    'jawai-vs-ranthambore-which-safari' => [
      ['The difference in what you are chasing', 'Ranthambore is a famous tiger reserve where tigers, forts and lakes make a dramatic setting, but sightings depend on zones, quotas and queue-style park entry. Jawai is about leopards on open granite hills, private jeeps and flexible timing. One is a classic national park experience; the other is a quieter, more personal one.'],
      ['Cost, crowds and booking', 'Ranthambore requires advance permits for specific zones and shared or private vehicles in a busy park. Jawai uses private jeeps and local trackers, with fewer vehicles and a slower pace. Both fill up in peak winter. Compare real quotes with the same inclusions. See [Jawai safari price explained](blog/jawai-safari-price-per-person-per-jeep-explained).'],
      ['Who should pick which', '- Choose Ranthambore if tigers are your dream and you accept park rules and crowds. || - Choose Jawai if you want leopards, photography freedom and a calmer atmosphere. || - Do both if time allows, since they are in different parts of Rajasthan. || Whatever you pick, plan multiple drives and keep expectations realistic, because wild animals cannot be scheduled.']],

    'wildlife-of-jawai-beyond-leopards' => [
      ['Reading the day’s activity', 'Dawn and dusk bring the most movement: jackals returning from the fields, peafowl calling from rocks, langurs coming down to drink, crocodiles basking as the sun warms the sandbars. Midday is quiet, and a good guide uses it to explain tracks, plants and rock formations. Learning to notice small signs turns every drive into a richer experience.'],
      ['Wildlife and the seasons', 'Winter brings migratory birds and cold, clear mornings. Summer concentrates animals around remaining water. The monsoon brings frogs, insects, blooming scrub and new growth that attracts herbivores. Each season has a different cast, which is why returning visitors rarely see the same Jawai twice. Read the [animals in Jawai checklist](blog/animals-you-can-see-in-jawai-wildlife-checklist).'],
      ['Tips for seeing more', '- Choose a tracker who knows the area well. || - Stay quiet and still, and listen for alarm calls. || - Carry binoculars and a field guide or app. || - Do several drives, with different timings. || - Be patient and curious. The small things often turn out to be the highlights. See [birds of Jawai](blog/birds-of-jawai-checklist-by-season).']],

    'birdwatching-in-jawai' => [
      ['The best birding spots and times', 'The dam shoreline is the main draw, with mudflats and shallows that attract waders and ducks, especially in winter. The scrub on the hills hosts shrikes, babblers and chats, while farm edges bring doves, bee-eaters and peafowl. Birding is best at first light and in the late afternoon, when birds are most active and the light is soft.'],
      ['What you need', '- Binoculars (8x42 is a good all-round choice). || - A bird guide or apps such as Merlin and eBird. || - A hat, water and layers for cold mornings. || - A notebook or phone to log what you see. || - Patience and quiet. || See our [birds of Jawai checklist](blog/birds-of-jawai-checklist-by-season).'],
      ['Combining birds with a leopard trip', 'A dawn drive often passes birds on the way, and a stop at the dam is easy to fit before breakfast. Ask your tracker to point out calls and plumage. Winter months from November to February give the longest species lists. For photographers, see [best camera and lens for Jawai](blog/best-camera-lens-and-settings-for-jawai-safari).']],

    'jawai-safari-cost-booking-what-is-included' => [
      ['Questions to ask about the price', '- How many guests does the jeep carry? || - How long is the drive, and what time does it start? || - Is there a trained local tracker with the vehicle? || - Are there any entry or permit fees? || - Is hotel pickup included? || These answers tell you whether two quotes are really comparable. See [Jawai safari price explained](blog/jawai-safari-price-per-person-per-jeep-explained).'],
      ['What you pay for beyond the jeep', 'A good safari is more than a vehicle: local knowledge, a driver who respects the animals, a clean, safe jeep and a plan that adapts to what is seen. Cheaper options may cut these. A little extra for a trained tracker and small group often pays off in better sightings and a calmer day.'],
      ['Booking without surprises', 'Confirm the rate, inclusions and timing in writing, and ask about changes and weather. We share rates on enquiry because they depend on season and plan. Read [how to book a Jawai leopard safari](blog/how-to-book-jawai-leopard-safari-step-by-step) and [booking tips](blog/jawai-trip-booking-tips-changes-cancellations).']],

    'jawai-wildlife-photography-tips' => [
      ['Thinking in light and patience', 'The best Jawai photographs come from waiting for the right moment, not racing around. Position the jeep so the light falls on the animal, wait for it to raise its head or move to a better rock, and have your settings ready. Early and late light gives warm colours and shadows that shape the fur and the granite. Midday light is harsh, so use it for details or rest.'],
      ['Beyond the leopard', 'Jackals in the fields, peafowl on rocks, birds at the dam and herders against the hills all make strong pictures. Landscape frames with a tiny animal show the scale of the hills. Portraits of people need consent and warmth. See [photographing granite landscapes](blog/photographing-granite-landscapes-and-golden-light-in-jawai).'],
      ['After the trip', 'Back up your photos each night, cull ruthlessly and edit gently. Resist the urge to share exact locations of sensitive sightings. For a full kit and settings guide, read [best camera, lens and settings](blog/best-camera-lens-and-settings-for-jawai-safari) and [mobile phone wildlife photography](blog/mobile-phone-wildlife-photography-on-safari).']],

    'rajasthani-vegetarian-food-to-eat-in-jawai' => [
      ['A day of eating in Bera', 'Start with chai and a light breakfast such as poha or a paratha. Lunch can be a village thali with bajra roti, dal, kadhi and a seasonal vegetable. In the evening, try tandoor snacks or dal baati churma, and finish with a sweet such as moong dal halwa or malpua. Drink plenty of buttermilk or lassi to balance the richness. See [dal baati churma guide](blog/dal-baati-churma-and-marwari-thali-guide).'],
      ['Ordering tips', '- Ask for mild or medium spice if you are unused to chilli. || - Share dishes to try more. || - Ask for less ghee or oil if you prefer lighter food. || - Tell us in advance about Jain or other dietary needs. See [Jain and pure vegetarian food](blog/jain-and-pure-vegetarian-food-in-jawai). || - Carry snacks for early drives.'],
      ['The story behind the food', 'Rajasthani cooking grew up in a dry land, using dried vegetables, millets, lentils, ghee and yogurt that keep well without refrigeration. Dishes such as ker sangri and gatte are as much about ingenuity as flavour. Read [Marwari vs Mewari food](blog/marwari-vs-mewari-food-differences-explained) and [Rajasthani sweets](blog/rajasthani-sweets-ghevar-malpua-churma-ladoo).']],

    'jawai-with-kids-family-travel-tips' => [
      ['Preparing children before the drive', 'Tell children what to expect: early starts, quiet voices, staying seated and looking carefully. Give them a small checklist of animals and birds to tick off, and let them hold a pair of binoculars. Children who know the rules and have a job usually enjoy the drive much more than those who are just told to be quiet.'],
      ['Age-by-age notes', '- Under 5: keep drives short, use a carrier for babies and plan around naps. See [Jawai with toddlers and babies](blog/jawai-with-toddlers-and-babies-tips). || - 6 to 12: the best age for engagement; bring binoculars and a notebook. || - Teenagers: give them a camera or a phone for photos and let them choose a drive time.'],
      ['Making it fun on the ground', 'Visit a village, try kulhad chai, spot birds at the dam and look for pugmarks. Add stargazing after dinner. Keep a flexible plan and an easy afternoon, because tired children make for unhappy drives. Read [is Jawai safe](blog/is-jawai-safe-for-tourists-safety-tips).']],

    'romantic-getaway-jawai-couples-honeymoon' => [
      ['A slow, quiet kind of romance', 'Jawai’s romance is not about nightlife. It is long golden evenings, a shared jeep at dawn, silence on a rock and a candle-lit meal under a huge sky. Plan for time together rather than a packed schedule, and leave afternoons free for rest, reading and chai.'],
      ['A three-night couples plan', '- Night 1: arrive, rest, an evening drive and a quiet dinner. || - Day 2: a dawn drive, a lazy breakfast, a village walk and sunset at the dam. || - Day 3: stargazing, a private table in the restaurant and a last dawn drive. See [anniversary and birthday ideas](blog/anniversary-and-birthday-trip-to-jawai-ideas).'],
      ['Practical tips for honeymooners', 'Tell us if you are celebrating and we will help with small touches. Pack warm layers for winter dawns and avoid bright clothes. Be flexible about sightings, since the wild cannot be promised, and consider adding Udaipur before or after for a city-and-wilderness contrast. See [Udaipur in one day](blog/udaipur-sightseeing-after-jawai-safari).']],

    'day-trips-from-jawai-ranakpur-kumbhalgarh-mount-abu' => [
      ['Choosing the right day trip for you', '- Love architecture and quiet: Ranakpur. || - Love history and views: Kumbhalgarh. || - Want cool air and a lake: Mount Abu. || - Want a city day: Udaipur or Jodhpur. || Match the trip to your energy. After an early drive, a short outing to Ranakpur is easier than a long one to Mount Abu.'],
      ['How to combine places sensibly', 'Ranakpur and Kumbhalgarh pair naturally on one long day. Mount Abu is better on its own. Udaipur and Jodhpur are better visited as the beginning or end of your trip rather than as day trips. See the detailed guides: [Ranakpur](blog/ranakpur-jain-temples-visit-guide-from-jawai), [Kumbhalgarh](blog/kumbhalgarh-fort-visit-guide-from-jawai) and [Mount Abu](blog/mount-abu-day-trip-from-jawai).'],
      ['Practical points', 'Start early, carry water, wear comfortable shoes and modest clothes for temples. Confirm opening hours, since some temples have different visitor times. Hire a car with a driver for the day, and plan to be back by dusk. Ask us on [WhatsApp](contact) and we will help you arrange it.']],

    'jawai-safari-rules-etiquette-responsible-travel' => [
      ['Why each rule exists', 'Staying seated keeps you and the animal safe. Keeping voices low lets trackers hear alarm calls and lets the animal behave normally. No flash prevents startling a leopard. No feeding stops animals associating people with food. Staying on tracks protects the habitat. Every rule is simple, and each protects either people, leopards or both.'],
      ['What to do if something goes wrong', 'If an animal moves close to the jeep, stay calm, stay seated and let the driver handle it. If someone feels unwell, tell the driver quietly. If you see someone breaking the rules, speak to your guide rather than confronting them. The tracker knows how to handle each situation.'],
      ['Being a good visitor beyond the safari', '- Respect village space and privacy. || - Take your rubbish with you. || - Use refillable bottles. || - Buy local and tip fairly. || - Share responsible practices when you review or post. See [responsible wildlife tourism tips](blog/responsible-wildlife-tourism-tips-for-india).']],

    'private-parties-events-celebrations-in-jawai-bera' => [
      ['Planning a celebration step by step', '- Choose your date and guest count. || - Decide on the format: meal, hall hire, safari add-on or a combination. || - Share your budget and dietary needs. || - Confirm food, decorations and any music rules. || - Book rooms for guests who are staying overnight. || The earlier you tell us, the better we can plan.'],
      ['What suits Jawai best', 'Jawai suits small, personal gatherings: birthdays, anniversaries, family reunions, small weddings and team outings. The peaceful setting, vegetarian food and the option to add a safari make it feel different from a city banquet. Large, loud events may not fit the village setting, so tell us what you have in mind. See [group trips and offsites](blog/group-trip-and-corporate-offsite-in-jawai).'],
      ['Making it smooth', 'Confirm timings with the team, brief guests on safari etiquette and keep a rest slot for older relatives. For a milestone celebration, read [anniversary and birthday ideas](blog/anniversary-and-birthday-trip-to-jawai-ideas), and [request an event](book?type=event) or message us on [WhatsApp](contact).']],
    ];
}

/** FAQ blocks for the first 18 articles (slug => [[question, answer], ...]); also becomes FAQ structured data. */
function jm_blog_faq_1(): array {
    return [
    'jawai-leopard-safari-complete-guide' => [['How long is a Jawai leopard safari?', 'About three hours per drive, in a private open jeep with a local tracker, at dawn and dusk.'], ['Will I definitely see a leopard?', 'No honest operator can promise it. Jawai is known for visible leopards, and three or four drives raise your chances.'], ['What time do the drives start?', 'Around first light, roughly 6:00 AM, and about 4:00 PM for the evening drive. Timings shift with the season.']],
    'best-time-to-visit-jawai' => [['What is the best month to visit Jawai?', 'November to February for comfort, and March to June for long leopard sightings. It depends on your priority.'], ['Is Jawai open in the monsoon?', 'Yes. The hills are green and quiet, though heavy rain can limit some tracks.'], ['When is Jawai most crowded?', 'Winter weekends, Diwali, Christmas and New Year. Book early for those dates.']],
    'how-to-reach-jawai-from-udaipur-jodhpur-jaipur' => [['How far is Jawai from Udaipur?', 'About 114 km by road to Bera, roughly two hours, and the airport is about 129 km away.'], ['Which railway station is nearest?', 'Sumerpur (Jawai Bandh) is roughly 18 km away. Falna is a larger junction a little further.'], ['Can the hotel arrange a pickup?', 'Yes. Share your arrival details and we can help arrange a car and driver.']],
    'where-to-stay-in-jawai-bera-hotels-camps' => [['Where should I stay for a Jawai safari?', 'Somewhere in or near Bera, so you reach the hills quickly. Compare camps, homestays and hotels by comfort and honest reviews.'], ['Is a hotel better than a tented camp?', 'A hotel offers predictable comfort, which suits families and older guests. Camps feel closer to nature but can be cold or hot.'], ['Do hotels arrange safaris?', 'Many do. Ask what is included, who the tracker is and how drives are priced.']],
    'jawai-dam-jawai-bandh-history-birds-crocodiles' => [['Are there crocodiles in Jawai Dam?', 'Marsh crocodiles are known to live around the reservoir. Watch them from a distance and stay out of the water.'], ['When are birds best at the dam?', 'December to February, when migratory birds are present, though many species stay all year.'], ['Can I swim in the dam?', 'No. It is unsafe and not appropriate around crocodiles.']],
    'rabari-community-jawai-culture-and-leopards' => [['Who are the Rabari?', 'A traditional pastoral community who raise sheep, goats and camels and know the hills very well.'], ['Can I photograph Rabari people?', 'Ask first, and do not pay for pictures. Buying chai or a small item is a kinder way to say thanks.'], ['Why do leopards live near villages here?', 'The rocky hills offer cover, and local tolerance helps. See the article on coexistence for more.']],
    'jawai-3-day-itinerary-safari-kumbhalgarh-ranakpur' => [['Is three days enough for Jawai?', 'It gives four drives and a day trip, which many guests find ideal.'], ['Can I do Ranakpur and Kumbhalgarh in one day?', 'Yes, they pair naturally, but plan an early start and return before dark.'], ['What if I only have two days?', 'See our weekend itinerary for a shorter plan.']],
    'jawai-vs-ranthambore-which-safari' => [['Can I see tigers at Jawai?', 'No. Jawai is leopard country. For tigers, visit a tiger reserve such as Ranthambore.'], ['Which is cheaper, Jawai or Ranthambore?', 'It depends on season and operator. Compare quotes with the same inclusions.'], ['Which is better for photography?', 'Jawai’s private jeeps and open hills suit patient photographers, while Ranthambore offers tigers and dramatic settings.']],
    'wildlife-of-jawai-beyond-leopards' => [['What other animals live in Jawai?', 'Golden jackals, palm squirrels, langurs, marsh crocodiles and many birds, among others.'], ['Are there snakes?', 'Yes, but they avoid people. Wear closed shoes and stay on paths.'], ['Do I need binoculars?', 'They help a lot, both for leopards on distant rocks and for birds.']],
    'birdwatching-in-jawai' => [['What is the best time for birdwatching in Jawai?', 'November to February, when winter visitors are at the dam.'], ['Do I need a bird guide?', 'A field guide or an app such as Merlin helps, and your tracker can point out calls.'], ['Can I birdwatch and do a leopard safari together?', 'Yes. Many dawn drives stop at the water for birds.']],
    'jawai-safari-cost-booking-what-is-included' => [['Is the safari price per person?', 'Usually it is per private jeep, which carries up to about six guests.'], ['What is normally included?', 'The vehicle, driver and a local tracker. Ask about fees, pickup and refreshments.'], ['How do I book?', 'Send a request or message us on WhatsApp. We confirm availability and the rate personally.']],
    'jawai-wildlife-photography-tips' => [['What lens do I need for Jawai?', 'A 100–400mm or 200–500mm telephoto covers most sightings.'], ['What shutter speed should I use?', 'Around 1/1000 s or faster for moving animals, and 1/500 s for resting ones.'], ['Can I use a drone?', 'Usually not around wildlife. Ask before you bring one.']],
    'rajasthani-vegetarian-food-to-eat-in-jawai' => [['Is the food in Bera vegetarian?', 'Our kitchen is 100% vegetarian, with Rajasthani village food and tandoor dishes.'], ['Can I get mild food?', 'Yes. Ask for mild or medium spice and less oil.'], ['Is Jain food available?', 'Yes, on request. Tell us when you book.']],
    'jawai-with-kids-family-travel-tips' => [['Is Jawai suitable for children?', 'Yes, with a private jeep, short drives and a plan around naps and meals.'], ['What age is best for a safari?', 'Children of about six and older usually enjoy it most, but younger children can come with care.'], ['What should kids wear?', 'Neutral colours, warm layers in winter, a hat and closed shoes.']],
    'romantic-getaway-jawai-couples-honeymoon' => [['Is Jawai good for a honeymoon?', 'Yes, if you like quiet landscapes, sunsets and nature rather than nightlife.'], ['Can you arrange something special?', 'Tell us the occasion when you book and we will help with small touches.'], ['How many nights should we stay?', 'Three nights is comfortable for drives, rest and a little exploration.']],
    'day-trips-from-jawai-ranakpur-kumbhalgarh-mount-abu' => [['Which day trip is best from Jawai?', 'Ranakpur is closest and easiest. Kumbhalgarh suits history lovers, and Mount Abu suits a cooler break.'], ['Can I visit two places in one day?', 'Ranakpur and Kumbhalgarh pair well. Others are better on separate days.'], ['Can the hotel arrange a car?', 'Yes. Message us and we will help arrange a driver for the day.']],
    'jawai-safari-rules-etiquette-responsible-travel' => [['Can I get out of the jeep?', 'No. Stay seated unless your tracker clearly says it is safe.'], ['Is flash photography allowed?', 'No. It can startle animals.'], ['Can I feed animals?', 'Never. Feeding harms wildlife and creates danger.']],
    'private-parties-events-celebrations-in-jawai-bera' => [['Do you host parties at the hotel?', 'Yes, small celebrations such as birthdays, anniversaries and family functions, subject to availability.'], ['Can you cater to special diets?', 'Yes. Tell us your needs in advance.'], ['Can we add a safari to the event?', 'Yes, drives can be arranged for guests alongside the celebration.']],
    ];
